[[Open This|Replatform]]

---

Replatforming is a cloud migration strategy within the 6Rs framework that involves making limited optimizations to applications during migration, without changing their core architecture.

Often referred to as ''“[[Lift, Tinker, and Shift]],”'' Replatform enables organizations to move applications to the cloud while taking advantage of some cloud-native features—such as managed databases or containerization—without a full redesign.

When to use replatforming: Replatforming is ideal for organizations that want to gain immediate cloud benefits (like improved performance or scalability) but do not have the time or resources for a complete application refactor. It is also suitable for businesses aiming to reduce operational overhead by adopting managed services.

Advantages of replatforming:

Speed: Faster than full refactoring while still enabling some improvements.
Cost optimization: Can reduce operational costs by leveraging managed cloud services.
Improved performance: Takes advantage of select cloud-native capabilities.

Limitations of replatforming:

Partial optimization: Does not fully leverage the cloud’s potential compared to refactoring.
Compatibility constraints: Some legacy components may limit how much optimization is possible.
Incremental complexity: Introduces some changes that require testing and adjustment.

Step-by-step guide for replatforming:

* [[Replatform Assessment]]: Identify applications that would benefit from minor optimizations, such as switching to managed databases or container platforms.
* [[Replatform Planning]]: Define which components will be modified and choose appropriate cloud services (e.g., managed databases, app services).
* [[Replatform Preparation]]: Prepare the cloud environment, including setting up platform services like databases, containers, or middleware.
* [[Replatform Modification]]: Make targeted changes to the application, such as updating configurations or replacing certain components with managed services.
* [[Replatform Migration]]: Move the application and data to the cloud, incorporating the planned optimizations.
* [[Replatform Testing]]: Validate application functionality, integration, and performance after migration.
* [[Replatform Optimization]]: Fine-tune configurations and services to further improve cost efficiency and performance.